WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.080
This strip of paper cards is a model

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:04.160
of twelve base pairs of DNA.

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:07.000
It can't do anything a real cell does.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:08.320
So why build it?

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:11.520
Because moving every piece yourself makes you follow

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:13.520
the same rules the cell follows.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:16.880
In this practical you'll build two models, one

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:19.280
of DNA copying and one of making a

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:22.240
polypeptide, and then judge what each one gets

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:24.000
right and what it gets wrong.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:28.720
Here's the aim: build models of DNA replication

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:32.080
and polypeptide synthesis, and assess how well they

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:33.920
represent the real processes.

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:37.760
You need card in four colours, scissors, sticky

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:40.960
tape, a pencil, a handful of pop-beads and

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:42.800
three small paper cups.

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:46.400
Blue card is original DNA, green is new

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:50.240
DNA, yellow is RNA, and orange cards are

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:51.880
transfer RNAs.

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:54.120
The beads are amino acids.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:56.240
Keep the colours the same all the way

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:58.400
through, because they let you track old and

00:00:58.480 --> 00:00:59.760
new strands.

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:02.240
The only hazard is the scissors, so cut

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:03.040
on the desk.

00:01:04.267 --> 00:01:07.026
Step one: make the nucleotides.

00:01:07.307 --> 00:01:10.746
Each card is one nucleotide: draw a phosphate

00:01:10.787 --> 00:01:12.506
and a sugar on the strip, and the

00:01:12.587 --> 00:01:14.066
tab is the base.

00:01:14.347 --> 00:01:16.506
Cut the tabs so that A only fits

00:01:16.667 --> 00:01:19.226
T, and C only fits G.

00:01:19.467 --> 00:01:21.186
That shape rule is the heart of the

00:01:21.227 --> 00:01:24.426
model, because in real DNA only those partners

00:01:24.587 --> 00:01:27.306
fit and hold together with hydrogen bonds.

00:01:27.467 --> 00:01:30.106
So give A and T tabs two notches,

00:01:30.187 --> 00:01:32.826
and C and G tabs three, one notch

00:01:32.907 --> 00:01:34.106
per hydrogen bond.

00:01:35.334 --> 00:01:37.413
While you're cutting, make the pieces for the

00:01:37.494 --> 00:01:38.853
second model too.

00:01:39.174 --> 00:01:42.613
Cut yellow RNA cards exactly like the DNA

00:01:42.694 --> 00:01:45.733
cards, but write U wherever you'd write T.

00:01:46.374 --> 00:01:48.453
A U tab gets the same two notches

00:01:48.534 --> 00:01:50.293
as T, so it fits A.

00:01:50.934 --> 00:01:54.453
For each transfer RNA, cut an orange T-shaped

00:01:54.534 --> 00:01:56.773
card with three base tabs along its bottom

00:01:56.854 --> 00:01:57.253
edge.

00:01:57.734 --> 00:01:59.973
Those three tabs are its anticodon.

00:02:00.454 --> 00:02:02.293
Glue a paper cup on top to hold

00:02:02.374 --> 00:02:03.093
one bead.

00:02:03.494 --> 00:02:05.853
You need three: U A C with a

00:02:05.894 --> 00:02:08.453
red bead, C G U with a gold

00:02:08.534 --> 00:02:10.933
bead, and A A A with a green

00:02:11.014 --> 00:02:13.653
bead, matched using a codon chart.

00:02:13.974 --> 00:02:16.773
Last, cut a ribosome shape from card, with

00:02:16.854 --> 00:02:19.573
a window exactly three tabs wide, so it

00:02:19.654 --> 00:02:21.573
shows one codon at a time.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:23.520
Step two:

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:25.600
build the starting molecule.

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:28.960
Tape twelve blue cards into a row, backbone

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:31.040
to backbone, in this order.

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:33.920
Then pair a second blue strand against it,

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:35.120
tab to tab.

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:37.680
Draw a five prime to three prime arrow

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:40.320
along each strand, and point the second arrow

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:44.000
the other way, because DNA is antiparallel.

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:46.880
The tape holds each backbone together, like strong

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:50.240
covalent bonds, while the tabs only touch, like

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:52.720
the weaker hydrogen bonds between bases.

00:02:53.934 --> 00:02:54.653
Step three:

00:02:55.214 --> 00:02:55.773
copy it.

00:02:56.254 --> 00:02:58.493
Pull the two strands apart at the tabs.

00:02:58.894 --> 00:03:01.533
The tape stays intact, just as the backbones

00:03:01.614 --> 00:03:03.693
do when the hydrogen bonds break.

00:03:04.014 --> 00:03:06.533
Now each blue strand is a template.

00:03:06.814 --> 00:03:09.133
Add green cards one at a time, each

00:03:09.214 --> 00:03:12.013
opposite its only matching partner, and tape the

00:03:12.094 --> 00:03:12.813
new backbone.

00:03:13.294 --> 00:03:15.053
You end up with two molecules.

00:03:15.454 --> 00:03:17.773
Each one has one blue strand and one

00:03:17.854 --> 00:03:18.813
green strand.

00:03:20.034 --> 00:03:22.113
Now record what the model shows.

00:03:22.354 --> 00:03:25.073
Draw both molecules and label the old and

00:03:25.154 --> 00:03:26.193
new strands.

00:03:26.434 --> 00:03:28.993
Then read each one against the original.

00:03:29.234 --> 00:03:31.793
They match base for base, because every new

00:03:31.874 --> 00:03:33.833
card was set by its template.

00:03:34.114 --> 00:03:36.433
Try forcing a wrong card in: it won't

00:03:36.514 --> 00:03:39.153
sit flat, so a mismatch stands out.

00:03:39.474 --> 00:03:41.873
One original strand in each molecule is the

00:03:41.954 --> 00:03:43.473
semi-conservative result.

00:03:43.954 --> 00:03:46.433
In a dividing cell, these two copies stay

00:03:46.594 --> 00:03:49.553
joined as the sister chromatids that mitosis later

00:03:49.634 --> 00:03:50.433
separates.

00:03:51.634 --> 00:03:54.753
Step four switches to the second model: making

00:03:54.834 --> 00:03:55.953
a polypeptide.

00:03:56.434 --> 00:03:58.793
Unzip the whole gene, and use only the

00:03:58.834 --> 00:03:59.993
template strand.

00:04:00.194 --> 00:04:03.073
Build an RNA strand from yellow cards, pairing

00:04:03.154 --> 00:04:06.273
as before, except that RNA has U instead

00:04:06.354 --> 00:04:08.993
of T, so a yellow U sits opposite

00:04:09.074 --> 00:04:10.113
every A.

00:04:10.194 --> 00:04:12.433
Then peel the yellow strand off and close

00:04:12.514 --> 00:04:13.953
the DNA back up.

00:04:14.034 --> 00:04:16.673
That yellow strip is your messenger RNA, and

00:04:16.714 --> 00:04:18.673
the gene itself is unchanged.

00:04:19.900 --> 00:04:22.220
Step five: translation.

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:25.980
Slide the messenger RNA through a paper ribosome,

00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:28.460
which shows three bases at a time, one

00:04:28.540 --> 00:04:29.300
codon.

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:32.540
Each orange card carries one bead, its amino

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:36.139
acid, and three tabs, its anticodon, that fit

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:36.860
its codon.

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:39.820
Match the first card to the start codon,

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:41.180
A, U, G.

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:43.580
Then match the next, and clip its bead

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:44.380
to the first.

00:04:44.780 --> 00:04:47.100
Keep going until a stop codon, where no

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:48.060
card fits.

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:50.060
Then the bead chain comes off.

00:04:51.267 --> 00:04:55.026
Record the results in a table: codon, anticodon

00:04:55.187 --> 00:04:57.906
and amino acid, using a codon chart.

00:04:58.227 --> 00:05:01.826
This gene gives A, U, G, then G,

00:05:01.987 --> 00:05:05.306
C, A, then U, U, U, then the

00:05:05.347 --> 00:05:07.346
stop codon U, G, A.

00:05:07.987 --> 00:05:12.386
That's methionine, alanine and phenylalanine: three amino acids

00:05:12.467 --> 00:05:13.346
joined in order.

00:05:13.987 --> 00:05:16.946
Three bases chose each bead, so changing one

00:05:17.027 --> 00:05:19.106
card in the DNA can change a bead

00:05:19.147 --> 00:05:19.986
in the chain.

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:22.880
Now assess the models.

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:25.040
Start with what they represent well.

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:27.520
The tab shapes show why A pairs with

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:29.360
T and C with G.

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:32.160
The colours show that each new molecule keeps

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:33.920
one original strand.

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:36.320
The ribosome window shows that the code is

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:39.040
read in threes, and the orange cards show

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:42.560
why transfer RNAs matter: each one links a

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:44.400
codon to its amino acid.

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:47.120
Then the limits.

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:50.160
Your hands did every job, so the model

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:53.520
hides the enzymes that unzip DNA, build new

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:57.760
strands and RNA, and load each transfer RNA.

00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:00.080
It's flat, so it hides the twist that

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:02.640
has to unwind before copying.

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:05.520
It's tiny, so it hides how long copying

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:09.440
takes: real genes run to thousands of bases.

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:11.600
Nothing makes new strands grow in the right

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:14.160
direction, so it hides the rule that cells

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:16.240
build only one way.

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:18.480
And it never shows a bead chain folding

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:21.120
into a working protein, so it hides why

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:23.360
the order of amino acids matters.

00:06:24.567 --> 00:06:26.966
A good assessment ends with improvements.

00:06:27.527 --> 00:06:29.926
Add new cards only at the three prime

00:06:30.007 --> 00:06:32.966
end, following each strand's arrow, as cells do.

00:06:33.607 --> 00:06:36.326
Add labelled cards for each enzyme job.

00:06:36.567 --> 00:06:38.486
And twist the finished ladder to show the

00:06:38.607 --> 00:06:39.366
helix.

00:06:39.687 --> 00:06:42.486
Each change fixes one limit you named, which

00:06:42.567 --> 00:06:44.566
is what makes it an improvement, not just

00:06:44.647 --> 00:06:45.526
decoration.

00:06:46.734 --> 00:06:47.373
Your turn.

00:06:47.934 --> 00:06:50.653
A student builds a replication model using white

00:06:50.734 --> 00:06:53.493
cards only, for both the original and the

00:06:53.534 --> 00:06:54.653
new strands.

00:06:54.974 --> 00:06:58.253
Both new molecules match the original exactly.

00:06:58.574 --> 00:07:01.453
Explain why this model can't show that replication

00:07:01.534 --> 00:07:04.573
is semi-conservative, and suggest one change.

00:07:05.134 --> 00:07:06.493
Pause here to answer.

00:07:16.900 --> 00:07:19.460
With one colour, old and new strands look

00:07:19.540 --> 00:07:21.700
the same, so you can't see that each

00:07:21.860 --> 00:07:24.340
new molecule keeps one original strand.

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:27.780
Use two colours, one for original and one

00:07:27.860 --> 00:07:28.420
for new.

00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:31.540
Then each molecule shows one of each.

00:07:31.860 --> 00:07:33.540
If you wrote that the model is fine

00:07:33.700 --> 00:07:37.700
because the sequences match, matching shows accurate copying,

00:07:37.780 --> 00:07:39.300
not which strand is old.

00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:43.860
So, to build and assess a model: decide

00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:46.820
what each piece stands for, follow the real

00:07:46.900 --> 00:07:49.460
rules so the model is valid, and record

00:07:49.540 --> 00:07:50.500
what you see.

00:07:50.820 --> 00:07:53.860
Then name what the model represents well, what

00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:56.020
it leaves out, and how to improve it.

00:07:56.820 --> 00:07:59.780
Next: how the chain of amino acids folds

00:07:59.900 --> 00:08:02.180
into a protein, and becomes a trait you

00:08:02.260 --> 00:08:02.980
can see.

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:05.640
Here are the key notes.

00:08:05.880 --> 00:08:07.800
Pause the video if you need more time

00:08:07.880 --> 00:08:08.680
to copy them.
